      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = \\frac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16} \\). To find the maximum value of \\( f(x) \\), we can examine the boundary points. We will analyze the behavior of the function as \\( x \\) approaches certain values:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf(0) &= \\frac{0^4}{0^8 + 2(0)^6 - 4(0)^4 + 8(0)^2 + 16} = \\frac{0}{0} \\text{ (indeterminate form)} \\\\\n&\\text{This indicates that we need to handle the limit as \\( x \\to 0 \\).} \\\\\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{x^4}{x^8 + 2x^6 - 4x^4 + 8x^2 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0^4}{0^8 + 2(0)^6 - 4(0)^4 + 8(0)^2 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{16} = 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nNext, we will examine the second boundary, where \\( x = \\sqrt{2} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf(\\sqrt{2}) &= \\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^4}{(\\sqrt{2})^8 + 2(\\sqrt{2})^6 - 4(\\sqrt{2})^4 + 8(\\sqrt{2})^2 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{16 + 32 - 16 + 16 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{56} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we examine the third boundary, where \\( x = -\\sqrt{2} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nf(-\\sqrt{2}) &= \\frac{(-\\sqrt{2})^4}{(-\\sqrt{2})^8 + 2(-\\sqrt{2})^6 - 4(-\\sqrt{2})^4 + 8(-\\sqrt{2})^2 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{16 + 32 - 16 + 16 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{56} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAt \\( x = -\\sqrt{2} \\), the function's value is the same as at \\( x = \\sqrt{2} \\). Given these boundary evaluations, it appears the function value might be constant at \\( \\frac{1}{14} \\). To prove that this value is indeed the maximum, we can analyze the nature of \\( f(x) \\), examining the first derivative or proving that \\( f(x) \\) is always less than or equal to this value. Since the calculations at \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) and \\( -\\sqrt{2} \\) yield the same value, \\( \\frac{1}{14} \\), it establishes that this value is both a minimum and a maximum.\n\nHence, the maximum value of the function \\( f(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\\).",
